1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,817
Aarav's ceiling fan hums, but
it will not start spinning.

2
00:00:03,817 --> 00:00:06,000
Give it a push, and off it goes.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:08,804
The electrician swaps one small box.

4
00:00:08,804 --> 00:00:13,561
Its label says two point five
microfarads, four hundred and forty volts.

5
00:00:13,561 --> 00:00:15,496
What do those numbers mean?

6
00:00:15,996 --> 00:00:18,393
Capacitors come in two big families.

7
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:19,765
Fixed and variable.

8
00:00:20,115 --> 00:00:23,633
A fixed capacitor has one
value that never changes.

9
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:26,444
Each type is named after its dielectric.

10
00:00:26,794 --> 00:00:29,489
Ceramic capacitors are small and cheap.

11
00:00:29,489 --> 00:00:34,301
They hold small values, in picofarads
and nanofarads, and they work well

12
00:00:34,301 --> 00:00:35,806
at high frequencies.

13
00:00:36,156 --> 00:00:40,676
Mica capacitors are very stable and
accurate, so they are used where the

14
00:00:40,676 --> 00:00:42,212
value must not drift.

15
00:00:42,562 --> 00:00:47,098
Paper and plastic film capacitors
are long sheets, rolled up tight.

16
00:00:47,098 --> 00:00:50,197
They hold nanofarads up
to a few microfarads.

17
00:00:50,547 --> 00:00:53,155
Electrolytic capacitors hold the most.

18
00:00:53,155 --> 00:00:58,018
From about one microfarad to thousands
of microfarads, in a small can.

19
00:00:58,368 --> 00:01:02,342
But an electrolytic capacitor
has a plus lead and a minus lead.

20
00:01:02,342 --> 00:01:04,687
It must go in the right way round.

21
00:01:05,337 --> 00:01:08,365
A variable capacitor lets
you change the value.

22
00:01:08,715 --> 00:01:13,424
In a gang capacitor, one set of metal
plates turns into the gaps of another

23
00:01:13,424 --> 00:01:14,324
set.

24
00:01:13,925 --> 00:01:15,485
Air is the dielectric.

25
00:01:15,835 --> 00:01:19,172
Remember, C equals K
epsilon nought A over d.

26
00:01:19,172 --> 00:01:23,881
Turning the knob changes the overlap
area A, so C changes with it.

27
00:01:24,581 --> 00:01:27,024
A trimmer is a tiny variable capacitor.

28
00:01:27,024 --> 00:01:30,335
You set it once, with a
screwdriver, and leave it.

29
00:01:30,985 --> 00:01:33,231
Let's tune a radio on the breadboard.

30
00:01:33,231 --> 00:01:36,695
The gang capacitor sits with
a coil, in a tuned circuit.

31
00:01:37,045 --> 00:01:38,137
Turn the knob.

32
00:01:38,137 --> 00:01:41,065
The plates overlap more,
and the area grows.

33
00:01:41,415 --> 00:01:45,595
The capacitance climbs from
fifty to four hundred picofarads.

34
00:01:45,945 --> 00:01:47,193
Watch the scope.

35
00:01:47,193 --> 00:01:50,127
As C goes up, the tuned wave slows down.

36
00:01:50,127 --> 00:01:54,959
Its frequency falls, from about fourteen
hundred to five hundred kilohertz.

37
00:01:55,309 --> 00:01:58,290
That is how one knob picks
one station at a time.

38
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:01,367
Now, the ratings printed on a capacitor.

39
00:02:01,717 --> 00:02:04,275
First, the capacitance and its tolerance.

40
00:02:04,275 --> 00:02:08,568
Just like a resistor, the real value
can differ from the marked value.

41
00:02:08,918 --> 00:02:10,832
Second, the working voltage.

42
00:02:10,832 --> 00:02:14,391
It is the highest voltage the
dielectric can safely take.

43
00:02:14,391 --> 00:02:17,508
Go above it, and the
dielectric can break down.

44
00:02:17,858 --> 00:02:22,320
So always choose a working voltage
higher than the voltage in your circuit.

45
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:26,048
Third, polarity, for
electrolytic capacitors.

46
00:02:26,048 --> 00:02:29,423
A stripe with minus signs
marks the negative lead.

47
00:02:29,423 --> 00:02:31,424
The longer lead is positive.

48
00:02:31,774 --> 00:02:35,297
Fit it backwards, and it can
heat up, leak, or even burst.

49
00:02:35,947 --> 00:02:40,858
So the fan label means two point five
microfarads, safe up to four hundred

50
00:02:40,858 --> 00:02:42,147
and forty volts.

51
00:02:42,797 --> 00:02:44,768
Value, voltage, polarity.

52
00:02:44,768 --> 00:02:46,058
Check all three.

53
00:02:46,858 --> 00:02:52,663
Small ceramic and film capacitors often
carry a three digit code, in picofarads.

54
00:02:53,013 --> 00:02:55,329
The first two digits are the value.

55
00:02:55,329 --> 00:02:57,894
The third digit is the number of zeros.

56
00:02:58,594 --> 00:03:01,156
A letter after it gives the tolerance.

57
00:03:01,156 --> 00:03:05,424
J is five percent, K is ten
percent, and M is twenty percent.

58
00:03:06,074 --> 00:03:07,294
Worked example.

59
00:03:07,294 --> 00:03:10,824
A ceramic capacitor is
marked four seven three J.

60
00:03:11,174 --> 00:03:13,632
Four, seven, then three zeros.

61
00:03:13,632 --> 00:03:15,916
Forty seven thousand picofarads.

62
00:03:16,616 --> 00:03:19,829
A thousand picofarads make one nanofarad.

63
00:03:19,829 --> 00:03:25,844
So that is forty seven nanofarads, or
zero point zero four seven microfarads.

64
00:03:26,544 --> 00:03:29,296
And J means plus or minus five percent.

65
00:03:29,946 --> 00:03:31,411
Now, the exam trap.

66
00:03:31,761 --> 00:03:36,778
Many students read one zero four
as one hundred and four picofarads.

67
00:03:36,778 --> 00:03:37,678
Wrong.

68
00:03:37,828 --> 00:03:40,342
The last digit counts the zeros.

69
00:03:40,342 --> 00:03:43,448
One zero four is ten, with four zeros.

70
00:03:43,448 --> 00:03:45,712
A hundred thousand picofarads.

71
00:03:45,712 --> 00:03:50,319
That is one hundred nanofarads,
or zero point one microfarads.

72
00:03:50,969 --> 00:03:52,547
Where is this asked?

73
00:03:52,547 --> 00:03:54,125
In PUC board papers.

74
00:03:54,125 --> 00:03:58,964
Name and compare the types, explain
the working voltage and polarity, or

75
00:03:58,964 --> 00:04:00,754
decode a capacitor code.

76
00:04:01,404 --> 00:04:02,304
Try this.

77
00:04:02,276 --> 00:04:05,564
A ceramic capacitor is
marked two two three K.

78
00:04:05,564 --> 00:04:07,043
What is its value?

79
00:04:12,443 --> 00:04:13,343
B.

80
00:04:12,896 --> 00:04:16,521
Twenty two nanofarads,
plus or minus ten percent.

81
00:04:16,871 --> 00:04:19,076
Two, two, then three zeros.

82
00:04:19,076 --> 00:04:21,218
Twenty two thousand picofarads.

83
00:04:21,568 --> 00:04:24,531
A thousand picofarads make one nanofarad.

84
00:04:24,531 --> 00:04:27,048
So that is twenty two nanofarads.

85
00:04:27,398 --> 00:04:29,958
And K means plus or minus ten percent.

86
00:04:30,608 --> 00:04:32,130
Screenshot this card.

87
00:04:35,330 --> 00:04:37,384
Next, inductors and inductance.

88
00:04:37,384 --> 00:04:40,350
Why does a coil fight
every change in current?
